      "id": 1,
      "text": "Stidham was named after George Washington Stidham, a prominent Creek Nation leader, settler, and judge who helped develop the region.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1,
        18
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stidham_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Members of the Muscogee Creek Nation originally settled Stidham following the Indian Removal Act of 1830.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stidham_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Stidham formally established itself as a rural hub for farming and ranching when it received its post office in 1897.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://grokipedia.com/page/stidham_oklahoma"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "Eufaula Lake, a major reservoir on the Canadian River, borders Stidham to the north, east, and southeast.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        21
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
